'''Jurruru''' is an [[language death|extinct]] [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian Aboriginal language]] formerly spoken in the [[Pilbara region of Western Australia|Pilbara region]] of [[Western Australia]], particularly near the [[Ashburton River (Western Australia)|Ashburton River]]. It is classified as part of the [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan language]] family, within the [[Ngayarda languages|Ngayarda]] subgroup.{{Sfn|Dench|1995|p=5}} There were two speakers left in 1967 and no speakers by 1986, leading to its extinction.{{Sfn|Thieberger|1993|p=18}}

== Grammar ==
Jurruru exhibits a [[nominative–accusative alignment]] system, characteristic of the Ngayarda languages to its north. This differs from the [[split-ergative]] nominal morphology found in the [[Kanyara languages|Kanyara]] and [[Mantharta language]]s. In Jurruru, the [[accusative case]] is marked by the suffix ''-ku'', contrasting with ''-nha'' in Mantharta and Kanyara. The [[allative case]] is marked by ''-karta'', distinct from ''-kurla''/''-rla'' in Mantharta and ''-pura''/''-kurrunu'' in Kanyara. The [[ablative case]] is marked by ''-nguru'', similar to one of the two ablatives used in [[Jiwarli dialect|Jiwarli]], but unlike the ''-parnti'' form seen in Kanyara and Mantharta.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=9}} Unlike the southern groups, Jurruru marks transitive object noun phrases consistently with the accusative ''-ku'' regardless of clause type. In Mantharta and Kanyara, object marking can vary depending on syntactic context, such as purpose or relative clauses.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=9}}

=== Pronouns ===
Jurruru's pronominal system aligns more closely with other Ngayarda languages, particularly Panyjima, than with the Mantharta group.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=9}} The first-person singular accusative/[[genitive case]] ''nganaju'' appears to be a cognate with forms in Jiwarli and [[Warriyangka language|Warriyangka]], though its function varies across groups.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=101}} Jurruru does not distinguish inclusive vs. exclusive forms in the first-person non-singular pronouns, a trait also found in the Kanyara language Payungu but not in Mantharta.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Austin |first=Peter K. |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/281939406_A_Reference_Grammar_of_the_Mantharta_Languages_Western_Australia |title=A Reference Grammar of the Mantharta Languages, Western Australia |date=July 2015 |publisher=[[University of London]] |pages=67}}</ref> Jurruru's plural pronouns may exhibit final long vowels (e.g., ''-kuu''), possibly deriving from earlier ''-kuru'' forms via loss of ''r''.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=12}}

=== Verbal morphology ===
Jurruru has a relatively simple verbal inflection system, consisting of two conjugation classes. This aligns with its northern Ngayarda neighbors and differs from the more complex systems found in the Mantharta and Kanyara languages.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=9}} Its verb roots reflect the old present-tense form (e.g., ''manku-'' "take", ''yanku-'' "go", ''yungku-'' "give", ''ngalku-'' "eat"), a feature shared across most Ngayarda languages except Palyku, Panyjima, Ngarla, and Nyamal.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=12}} By contrast, cognate verbs in Mantharta languages appear as ''mana-'' and ''yana-'' for "take" and "go," respectively.{{Sfn|Austin|1988|p=12}}

